Put the cursor in the address bar on Ctrl+T.

With Chromium's own UI, Ctrl+T opens a tab and leaves the focus nowhere:
typing does nothing until the address bar is clicked. Desktop Chrome puts
the cursor there.

Upstream gets it from the page rather than the command --
WebContentsViewAura::SetInitialFocus asks FocusLocationBarByDefault,
which asks the delegate, which answers by looking at the tab's last
committed entry and asking whether it is a new tab page. A tab that has
just been created has no committed entry, and that arm returns false
before it looks at anything else. Why the timing works out differently
elsewhere is not established here, so this does not touch that path.

Doing it on the command instead keeps the change to the gesture that
wants it: Ctrl+T and New Tab in the menu, not every tab something opens
programmatically. Same call the Ctrl+L / Focus Address Bar command
makes, and user-initiated for the same reason -- somebody pressed a key.

diff --git a/chrome/browser/ui/browser_command_controller.cc b/chrome/browser/ui/browser_command_controller.cc
--- a/chrome/browser/ui/browser_command_controller.cc
+++ b/chrome/browser/ui/browser_command_controller.cc
@@ -748,5 +748,11 @@
     case IDC_NEW_TAB: {
       NewTab(browser_, NewTabTypes::kNewTabCommand);
+#if BUILDFLAG(IS_OHOS)
+      // The new tab page does not claim the address bar here the way it does
+      // on other platforms, and a new tab nobody can type into is not much
+      // of one.
+      FocusLocationBar(browser_);
+#endif
       break;
     }
     case IDC_NEW_TAB_TO_RIGHT: {
